{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "label_pos",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "reviewer_expectation_source",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The reviewer's expectation baseline (casual buyer vs. expert vs. brand-loyal-disappointed) plausibly shapes both the underlying experience/quality of product they engage with (a candidate treatment) and the review valence outcome; disappointed brand loyalists and expert baselines drive harsher judgments. It is a pre-existing attribute of the reviewer, not a mediator on a single treatment path, and it is not named in the query, so it surfaces a genuine common-cause candidate."
    },
    {
      "name": "product_category_type",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Product category plausibly influences both which quality/attribute drivers are relevant (the treatment domain) and the base rate of negative sentiment (e.g., media books/films attract more polarized valence). It is a stable pre-treatment characteristic, not on a single mediating path, and it was not named in the query."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": false,
  "treatment_columns": [],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "reviewer_expectation_source", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.5},
    {"name": "product_category_type", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.55}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.35,
  "rationale": "The query asks for causal attribution: which factors cause predominantly negative reviews (label_pos). A valid causal setup needs (a) an explicit TREATMENT -- the candidate cause whose effect is estimated -- and (b) confounders that cause BOTH the treatment and the outcome. Here NO treatment column is present. Both added columns are context/segmentation variables that plausibly act as common causes, but with no treatment defined, they cannot be confounders in the strict sense (a confounder is defined relative to a treatment). They function more as effect-modifiers or covariates. Since the decisive requirement -- a candidate cause (treatment) to attribute -- is absent, the causal estimation is not set up. The columns were surfaced from data rather than copied from the query, which is positive, but the attribution structure is incomplete, so confounder_quality is low."
}

